00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:25.270
Then we will have some live demonstrations
on the web and on the command line.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:30.950
What is QuickBLASTP, this is a faster alternative
for ordinary protein protein blast.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:36.280
It uses a specially pre-indexed database.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.369
It is designed for rapid matching of queries
and very similar database sequences.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:45.560
About 97% of the database sequences with 70%
or more identity to query are found.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.729
And 98% of the database sequences with 80%
or more identity to query are found.

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:55.880
This is compared to what you would get with
ordinary BLASTp, so it is less sensitive.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.920
Why do we need this?

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:04.790
There are challenges that are the result of
the advent of high throughput sequencing and

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.150
processing of genomes.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:13.650
The default database, nr, now contains greater
than 141 million sequences.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.680
It also has 51 billion letters.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:22.739
The doubling time is two years.

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:27.430
Many of you have experienced the fact that
ordinary protein blast searches are taking

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:28.950
longer than they used to.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:37.130
If you have a highly represented protein,
one with lots of copies in the database, a

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.730
search can take a few minutes.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.700
Modern projects generate large numbers of
protein sequences and they need to analyze

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:51.740
a lot of the protein coding regions and what
you're trying to do, maybe you have a metagenome

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:57.200
or genomic sequence, is find the strong matches
in the database, because many of those proteins

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.560
will have strong matches.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.129
We need a process to let you quickly identify
strong matches to a set of query sequences.

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:08.440
Sensitivity is less important.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:14.810
This is an oversimplified description of QuickBLASTP.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:20.040
The first part of the search, uses an alignment
free method.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:26.050
It makes an index of the five-mers of the
database and the query sequences and looks

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:34.550
them up in memory to find candidate matches
with good k-mer content compatibility.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:40.379
The second part is to take those candidate
matches to do a blast alignment.

00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:43.440
Doing that means you get a standard blastp
alignment and statistics.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.840
The first part uses an alignment free method.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:57.890
Here are some results that Tom in the group
presented at the NIH Research Festival recently.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.129
They are the kinds of things we are talking
about.

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:03.150
THey looked at a proteome of Chigell flexneri.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:09.290
This has 4799 proteins from a whole genome
shotgun assembly project.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:16.019
And another kind of proteome, from a transcriptome
shotgun assembly project for a tick, Amblyomma

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.350
sculptum, and 4246 proteins.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:25.259
Basically they did searches comparing those
against the protein nr database.

00:04:25.259 --> 00:04:29.020
They got information from that about how blastp
and kblastp perform.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:36.869
Kblatp is the name of the standalone program
that corresponds to QuickBLASTP.

00:04:36.869 --> 00:04:41.169
For BLASTp, the Shigella took 10 hours.

00:04:41.169 --> 00:04:44.259
And for Amblyomma it took 18 hours.

00:04:44.259 --> 00:04:52.259
In the corresponding cases QuickBLASTP, the
faster method, reduced the time by a tremendous

00:04:52.259 --> 00:04:53.259
amount.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:57.000
The speedup for Shigella was 13 times faster.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.520
And for Amblyomma it was about 20 times faster.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:07.259
This is a huge improvement in the speed in
which you can analyze a set of proteins.

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:12.580
This is looking at the number of hits found
at various percent identities.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:20.139
As you go down in percent identity, kBLASTp
does worse compared to BLASTp.

00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:29.110
The little circle is the 65% identity point
and that is a good point to say, below that

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:33.589
percent identity, QuickBLASTp is not as effective.

00:05:33.589 --> 00:05:38.039
For this set of proteins, it is not as dramatic
as it is for the next set.

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:40.779
These are the ones from the tick.

00:05:40.779 --> 00:05:45.159
You can see below 65% identity these depart
quite a bit.

00:05:45.159 --> 00:05:49.960
This has to do with what kinds of proteins
are in the database and how closely related

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.309
they are to the proteins we are working with.

00:05:52.309 --> 00:05:57.349
You see less saturation of BLASTp results
with the tick proteins than with the Shigella

00:05:57.349 --> 00:05:59.009
proteins.

00:05:59.009 --> 00:06:03.439
Another way to look at this, is to look at
the number of missed hits compared to BLASTp

00:06:03.439 --> 00:06:07.210
with a 65% identity cutoff and without one.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.580
You see fewer missed hits.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:13.050
The blue column is for zero missed hits.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.199
There's a slight difference in the 65% (orange)
bar.

00:06:17.199 --> 00:06:22.150
There's more difference if you look at that
for the tick proteins.

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:28.270
The take away is that this works well but
is only limited to things that are better

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:33.870
than 65% identity which is fine, if you are
trying to find out which protein is this in

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.650
the database.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:41.409
How can you run QuickBLASTP or kBLASTp?

00:06:41.409 --> 00:06:48.849
Smart blast has been around for a number of
years uses the QuickBLASTP algorithm already.

00:06:48.849 --> 00:06:52.699
QuickBLASTP is on the protein protein BLAST
page.

00:06:52.699 --> 00:07:00.729
KBLASTp is available as a stand-alone demonstration
package on the FTP area.

00:07:00.729 --> 00:07:14.090
In terms of the website, smart blast is linked
directly on the homepage.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:17.210
It uses the QuickBLASTP algorithm.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:21.119
If you want to know more about smart blast
we did a webinar on that a while back.

00:07:21.119 --> 00:07:24.729
That link will take you to our YouTube channel.

00:07:24.729 --> 00:07:31.229
This is the standard protein protein BLAST
page, you can see one of the program selections

00:07:31.229 --> 00:07:36.659
is now the QuickBLASTP.

00:07:36.659 --> 00:07:42.189
BLASTP is the default but you can change this
to QuickBLASTP.

00:07:42.189 --> 00:07:46.669
Right now this is only available for the nr
database.

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:57.839
It requires a special index of those five
Mers of the database.

00:07:57.839 --> 00:08:00.770
You can also download a Linux package for
this, quickblastp.tar.gz.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:10.459
This is available in the demo area of the
blast FTP area.

00:08:10.459 --> 00:08:12.339
There is a README and a tar ball there.

00:08:12.339 --> 00:08:16.539
We also have the indexing program as well.

00:08:16.539 --> 00:08:20.909
The README tells you exactly how to download
and install this.

00:08:20.909 --> 00:08:26.490
It comes with several different programs.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.270
This right now is only available for Linux.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:35.740
You get kBLASTp which is the corresponding
standalone version of QuickBLASTP.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:40.779
It does the first part and the second part
of the search.

00:08:40.779 --> 00:08:44.490
It has very similar options to BLASTp.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:53.520
There is the mkkblastindex, this is the program
that makes the database index, it requires

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:59.230
a formatted protein database as input and
it will add additional indices for the initital

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.180
alignment.

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:05.769
The package also comes with the update_blastdb.pl
Perl script for downloading your blast databases.

00:09:05.769 --> 00:09:16.750
There is also a script called catvolumes.sh
that loads the database into memory and you

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:18.940
can get a hot start on your search.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:25.120
It speeds up the search if you have a large
database.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:31.550
It specifies the refseq_protein database,
but you can modify this to use other databases.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:37.889
A couple of things to think about, we have
said this but I want to remind you, QuickBLASTP

00:09:37.889 --> 00:09:40.649
rapidly finds closely related proteins.

00:09:40.649 --> 00:09:49.370
It is not terribly useful if the matches are
below 65% identity.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:53.500
It is best used for batches of queries.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:10:01.760
Maybe smaller numbers on the web, but still
useful.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.470
The standalone program, kBLASTp.

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:09.730
For this to work it requires that the database
plus the indices be completely loaded into

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:11.279
RAM.

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:23.540
For the default nr database, the database
plus the indices require 200 to 250 GB of

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:24.540
RAM.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.889
The index files occupy more than half of this
total.

00:10:27.889 --> 00:10:33.050
You need a machine with lots of available
RAM to do this effectively.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:36.470
